    q: "You bought at a 5.0% cap on $1.0M of NOI and sold at a 4.4% cap after NOI grew to $1.21M. How much of the gain came from NOI growth and how much from the cap rate?",
    a: "$4.2M from NOI growth and $3.3M from the cap rate, a $7.5M gain on a $20.0M purchase. At the entry cap, $1.21M of NOI is worth $24.2M, so growth added $4.2M. Selling at 4.4% instead of 5.0% lifted that to $27.5M, another $3.3M. Growth is usually what the business plan earned; compression is what the market gave.",
    why: "Value is NOI ÷ cap rate, so any change in value splits into those two drivers, the way an LBO's return splits into EBITDA growth and multiple expansion. Investors care about the split because only one of them tends to repeat. NOI growth from leasing, renovation and expense control shows skill a sponsor can bring to the next deal; cap rate compression mostly reflects falling rates or rising demand, which can reverse. That's why investors discount track records built in a compressing market.",
