    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What's the difference between a trailing and a forward cap rate, and which is higher when rents are rising?",
    a: "A trailing cap rate uses the last 12 months of NOI; a forward cap rate uses the next 12 months'. When rents are rising, forward NOI is higher, so the forward cap rate is higher for the same price. Neither is wrong, but a cap rate and the NOI you apply it to must be measured the same way.",
    why: "Comps get quoted both ways, and mixing them misprices a deal. In a rising market, a comp's trailing cap rate is lower than the forward yield its buyer actually underwrote. Apply that low trailing cap rate to your forward NOI and you count growth twice: once in the NOI and again in the cap rate. The opposite mismatch undervalues, and in a falling market the gap flips.",
    example: "A comp sold for [[$20.0M]] on trailing NOI of [[$1.00M]] (5.0%) and forward NOI of [[$1.06M]] (5.3%). Your building's forward NOI is [[$1.50M]]. At the 5.3% forward cap it's worth about $28.3M; at the 5.0% trailing cap, $30.0M, overpaying by $1.7M, or 6%.",
    trap: "The same logic applies at exit: the sale is priced on the year after your hold, so an exit cap taken from trailing-NOI comps is too low for it."
