    classic: true,
    q: "How would you value an office building where the largest tenant's lease expires in 18 months?",
    a: "With a DCF that models the rollover, not a cap rate on today's NOI. Estimate the odds the tenant renews and, if it leaves, the downtime, free rent, TI and commissions to re-lease at market rent. Blend the outcomes or run each case, then price the risk with a higher discount rate or a lower bid.",
    why: "A cap rate on in-place NOI assumes that income continues, which is exactly what's in doubt. If the tenant leaves, you lose its rent during downtime, carry its share of taxes and operating costs, pay to re-lease and may re-let at a different rent, all within a few years, so the hit lands early and at nearly full value. Diligence narrows the range: talk to the tenant, learn whether it's growing or shrinking and check how much similar space sits empty nearby.",
    example: "The tenant pays [[$2.0M]] a year. If it leaves ([[40%]] odds): [[12]] months of lost rent ($2.0M) plus TI, commissions and free rent of [[$3.5M]], $5.5M in all. If it renews ([[60%]]): [[$1.0M]] of TI and commissions. Expected cost: 40% × $5.5M + 60% × $1.0M = $2.8M, before discounting and a risk cushion.",
    trap: "An expected value can hide the real risk: the building ends up worth one of two numbers, not the average. Lenders and buyers want to see the downside case on its own."
