    q: "How would you value a completely vacant building, and why might an owner-occupier pay more for it than an investor?",
    a: "An investor values it as if leased, then subtracts lease-up costs and a profit for the leasing risk, so its bid sits well below stabilized value. An owner-occupier compares the price with the rent it would otherwise pay. It takes no leasing risk and needs no leasing profit, so it can often pay more, especially for smaller buildings.",
    why: "A vacant building has no income to capitalize, so its value depends on who can use it. The investor has to find tenants, fund TI and commissions and carry the building meanwhile, and it wants to be paid for that risk. The user simply moves in: its return is the rent it stops paying, plus control of its space. That's why smaller vacant buildings often sell to users, and why land value or another use sets the floor when neither wants it.",
    example: "[[50,000]] SF at a market rent of [[$20]]/SF net: $1.0M of stabilized NOI, worth $14.3M at a [[7.0%]] cap. The investor deducts [[$1.5M]] of TI and commissions, [[$1.0M]] of carry and free rent and a [[15%]] profit ($2.1M): about $9.6M. A user saving $1.0M of rent a year, valued the same way, less [[$1.0M]] to fit out its space, could pay $13.3M.",
    trap: "Don't cap the pro forma NOI and stop: that's the as-stabilized value, not what the building is worth empty. For large buildings, few users need all the space, so investors usually set the price."
