    a: "Start from the sum of the parts (each property valued on its own), then adjust for a portfolio premium or discount. A portfolio earns a premium when it gives a large buyer scale it can't assemble otherwise. It trades at a discount when few buyers can write the check, or when weak assets come bundled with strong ones.",
    why: "Portfolio pricing comes down to the buyer pool. Large institutions may pay more to deploy capital in one trade, gain a foothold in a market or acquire an operating platform with its people and systems. But a bigger check means fewer bidders, and buyers who want only the best assets discount the rest or ask to cherry-pick. Whether premiums or discounts prevail shifts with the cycle, so sellers often weigh a single sale against selling in pieces.",
