    why: "P/FFO is a price-to-earnings multiple for the equity, so it mixes property pricing with financing. For an unlevered REIT, the FFO yield is roughly the cap rate less G&A as a share of value, here 4.75%, so P/FFO is about 21x. Debt that costs less than that yield raises FFO per dollar of equity and lowers the multiple; debt that costs more does the opposite. So a lower P/FFO isn't automatically cheaper. EV/EBITDA and implied cap rates strip out leverage, which makes them fairer comparisons.",
