    q: "What's the difference between market value and investment value?",
    a: "Market value is the most probable price in a competitive, arm's-length sale to a typical buyer; it's what an appraisal estimates. Investment value is what the property is worth to one particular buyer, given its cost of capital, financing, taxes and plans. A buyer should bid up to its investment value, and it wins when that beats everyone else's.",
    why: "Buyers differ. One may have cheaper capital, own the building next door, plan a redevelopment others can't or need to reinvest sale proceeds before a tax-deferral deadline. Each raises what that buyer can pay. Market value strips out such special circumstances so lenders, auditors and courts have a neutral number. In a competitive sale, the winning bid often reflects the most motivated buyer's investment value, which is why prices can land above appraisals.",
