    q: "Can a property's cap rate be higher than its discount rate? When?",
    a: "Yes, whenever its income is expected to shrink. Since cap rate ≈ discount rate − growth, negative growth pushes the cap rate above the discount rate. It happens with obsolete buildings losing tenants and with leases above market that will roll down. It's also true of a leasehold, whose value runs to zero when the ground lease ends.",
    why: "The cap rate is only the first year's yield; the discount rate is the whole annual return. When income grows, part of the return comes from growth, so the starting yield can sit below the discount rate. When income shrinks, the starting yield has to be higher to make up for the decline. A leasehold is the extreme case: its income stops at expiry, so it's valued as an annuity, not a perpetuity, and its cap rate climbs as the remaining term shortens.",
